 <section id="supplier"><h2>Supplier and agreement</h2><p>Charles Buckton trading as CCS Creative Technologies, UK sole trader. Business notices: 78 Seaton Way, Southport, PR9 9GP, United Kingdom. Business, support, security and privacy contact: <a href="mailto:ccscreativetech@gmail.com">ccscreativetech@gmail.com</a>.</p><p>The customer is the business identified in its onboarding order and workspace, represented by the authorised manager accepting these terms. The order records the customer’s legal/trading name, contact and authority, intended use, start date and any expressly agreed variations. The order and this dated schedule form the service agreement when both parties agree to live access. A manager must not accept on behalf of a business without authority. Retain a copy of these terms and your order. A later material change requires notice and agreement; a website edit alone does not retrospectively change an agreed order.</p></section>
-<section><h2>Draft Solo and Team release</h2><p>This schedule is prepared for review, not an active customer agreement. Existing sandbox tests remain on their prior configuration. A plan change does not erase records. A downgrade below existing staff records or staff access pauses editing while preserving manager viewing and exports; contact CCS before downgrading. Staff sign-in is unavailable on Solo. Upgrades and cancellations take effect according to Stripe’s confirmed subscription state; returning from a payment page alone does not change access. No automatic data deletion or additional trial is provided by a plan change.</p></section><section id="service"><h2>Service and total price</h2><p>One UK business workspace: customer records, assigned staff, finite recurring visits, checklists and completion notes, invoice/credit records, manual exports and protected business backups. Proposed subscriptions are <strong>Solo £7.99 total per month</strong> for one owner-operator, and <strong>Team £14.99 total per month</strong> with staff sign-in. Team 5 includes five people in total: one owner and up to four independent staff sign-ins. Its staff-record allowance is five, including the owner if recorded as a worker. These prices are not available as live subscriptions until commercial activation is approved. Proposed larger monthly bands for final review are Team 10 £24.99 (owner plus nine staff sign-ins), Team 15 £32.99 (owner plus fourteen), and Team 20 £39.99 (owner plus nineteen). Each has a staff-record allowance equal to its total people allowance. Businesses above twenty people require a tailored quote. Only an approved, configured band shown at checkout can be ordered; exceeding a limit never automatically buys an upgrade or charges extra. CCS is not registered for UK VAT (owner confirmation 23 October 2026); no VAT is added.</p><p>The service excludes online collection of your service customers’ payments, automatic customer invoice email, accounting integration, native app-store applications, unlimited storage, and medical, safeguarding or children’s records. Recording an invoice as paid does not move money. Restricted invoice-email testing with fictional records does not add customer sending to this scope. Any future email service requires separate approval and updated processing arrangements.</p><p>Your business retains rights in its records. Access does not transfer Quenvira source code, software ownership or documentation rights; applicable third-party licences remain in effect.</p></section>
+<section><h2>Draft Solo and Team release</h2><p>This schedule is prepared for review, not an active customer agreement. Existing sandbox tests remain on their prior configuration. A plan change does not erase records. A downgrade below the active staff-profile or independent sign-in allowance pauses routine editing while preserving manager viewing, exports and staff deactivation/access revocation. Inactive historical profiles remain in the records and do not consume the active-profile allowance. Reduce active profiles and sign-ins to fit before a downgrade takes effect; contact CCS for help. Staff sign-in is unavailable on Solo. The proposed plan-change policy, still subject to owner approval and end-to-end acceptance, is immediate upgrades with a prorated invoice and unchanged monthly billing date, cheaper plans at the end of the current period, and cancellation at period end. Stripe checkout or the portal must show the actual amount and effective date before confirmation. Paid editing requires the server to confirm a settled invoice and the current subscription state; returning from a payment page alone does not change access. No automatic data deletion or additional trial is provided by a plan change.</p></section><section id="service"><h2>Service and total price</h2><p>One UK business workspace: customer records, assigned staff, finite recurring visits, checklists and completion notes, invoice/credit records, manual exports and protected business backups. Proposed subscriptions are <strong>Solo £7.99 total per month</strong> for one owner-operator, and <strong>Team £14.99 total per month</strong> with staff sign-in. Team 5 includes five people in total: one owner and up to four independent staff sign-ins. Its active staff-profile allowance is five, including the owner if recorded as a worker; inactive historical profiles do not consume that allowance. These prices are not available as live subscriptions until commercial activation is approved. Proposed larger monthly bands for final review are Team 10 £24.99 (owner plus nine staff sign-ins), Team 15 £32.99 (owner plus fourteen), and Team 20 £39.99 (owner plus nineteen). Each has an active staff-profile allowance equal to its total people allowance, with independent staff sign-ins limited to one fewer than that total because the owner is included. Businesses above twenty people require a tailored quote. Only an approved, configured band shown at checkout can be ordered; exceeding a limit never automatically buys an upgrade or charges extra. CCS is not registered for UK VAT (owner confirmation 23 October 2026); no VAT is added.</p><p>The service excludes online collection of your service customers’ payments, automatic customer invoice email, accounting integration, native app-store applications, unlimited storage, and medical, safeguarding or children’s records. Recording an invoice as paid does not move money. Restricted invoice-email testing with fictional records does not add customer sending to this scope. Any future email service requires separate approval and updated processing arrangements.</p><p>Your business retains rights in its records. Access does not transfer Quenvira source code, software ownership or documentation rights; applicable third-party licences remain in effect.</p></section>
 <section id="trial"><h2>Trial, billing and cancellation</h2><p>The once-only app trial lasts 14 days from enrollment, requires no card and does not automatically convert to a paid subscription. Accepting service terms or creating a trial workspace starts no paid subscription. To subscribe, an authorised manager must deliberately accept the subscription terms and complete Stripe-hosted checkout. The checkout displays the billing dates before confirmation. If more than 48 trial hours remain, checkout preserves the original trial end; in its final 48 hours, wait until the trial ends before subscribing so free days are not lost.</p><p>A paid subscription renews monthly until cancelled through Manage subscription. Cancellation at period end retains access for the period paid. Failed, unpaid, paused or ended subscriptions do not allow routine editing. Viewing, exports and available access-revocation/recovery controls remain available for exit. Subscription status is verified on the server; returning from checkout alone grants no access. Contact CCS about billing disputes, duplicate or incorrect charges, or service failure. Mandatory legal remedies are unaffected.</p><p>Stripe handles card details; never enter card information in workspace records. End any running subscription before deleting the business. Minimal trial/billing mappings and acceptance records remain for administration and to prevent repeated free trials; workspace deletion does not erase Stripe’s records.</p></section>
 <section id="operation"><h2>Operation, support and limits</h2><p>Each account creates one initial business. The workspace record document is limited to 512 KiB. The encrypted backup format has a 20 MiB unencrypted limit and includes supported staff-photo files. Visits currently have one assigned worker and recurring schedules are finite. Check suitability and capacity before importing records.</p><p>Support is by business email. No 24-hour support, uptime, response-time or recovery-time guarantee, or automatic independent backup service is included. Managers must keep independent protected backups and keep their passwords separately. Accounts must remain secure; do not share sign-in links, bypass permissions, introduce malicious content or access another business’s records. Report security concerns promptly. CCS may restrict access needed to contain misuse or a security incident, while coordinating lawful record return and exit.</p></section>
 <section id="processing"><h2>Customer data-processing schedule</h2><p>The customer is controller of its business records. CCS is processor for operating them on the customer’s instructions, and a separate controller of its own account administration, security and business records, as explained in the <a href="/legal.html">privacy notice</a>.</p><p><strong>Subject, purpose and duration:</strong> hosting and operating service-business records during agreed access and the customer-directed return/deletion process. Processing includes storage, authorised display, scheduling, calculation, changes, export and deletion. People covered are adult staff/contractors and service customers/contact people. Data includes names, contact/service addresses, staff roles/capacity, optional portrait thumbnails, visits, notes/checklists, supplier/invoice records and access/operation metadata. Special-category, criminal-offence, clinical, safeguarding and children’s records are outside this service.</p><ol><li>CCS will process these records only on documented lawful customer instructions, including transfer instructions, unless applicable UK law requires otherwise; it will notify the customer of a legal requirement where permitted and flag instructions believed unlawful. Instructions and changes may be recorded by email. The customer supplies lawful instructions, appropriate notices and authority to enter records.</li><li>CCS will restrict processing access to authorised people under confidentiality duties and maintain security appropriate to the risks. Current controls include managed sign-in, server-enforced business/role separation, validated updates, conflict/idempotency checks and private photo routes. Managers administer access; staff see their own profile and assigned work without invoice prices or customer email. The disclosed manual-backup limitations apply.</li><li>CCS will assist the customer with individuals’ rights requests and applicable security, breach, impact-assessment and regulator-consultation duties, taking account of the processing and information available. It will notify the customer without undue delay after becoming aware of a personal-data breach and provide available facts and updates.</li><li>The customer generally authorises the hosting chain below for this service. CCS will impose equivalent processing obligations, remain responsible for its subprocessors’ performance, notify the customer of intended additions/replacements before affected processing, and allow a reasonable opportunity to object. Resolve an objection or arrange exit before affected processing continues.</li><li>CCS will provide information needed to demonstrate these obligations and allow and contribute to applicable customer or appointed-auditor audits and inspections, with reasonable confidentiality and security arrangements. This does not represent unrestricted physical access to third-party infrastructure or approval of a chargeable audit.</li><li>At service end, CCS will return or delete the records at the customer’s choice and delete existing copies unless UK law requires retention. The exit arrangements below apply to provider backups and copies outside active application storage.</li></ol>
 <h3>Provider chain and international processing</h3><p>OpenAI managed Sites supplies hosting and sign-in. For a UK individual subscriber, the published Sites DPA identifies OpenAI OpCo, LLC as the contracting provider. This app uses Cloudflare Workers, D1 and R2 through that hosting service. The provider’s published subprocessor disclosures include US web hosting and international support; they are not a guarantee of UK-only storage or a precise physical location for this workspace. Review the current <a href="https://openai.com/policies/chatgpt-sites-data-processing-addendum/">Sites DPA</a> and <a href="https://openai.com/policies/sub-processor-list/">provider list</a>. The DPA provides UK transfer provisions using the SCCs and UK Addendum where applicable. CCS makes no UK-only residency promise. Hosting data and content supplied in development conversations are distinct; do not put real customer records into development prompts.</p><p>Stripe receives the billing identity and payment information needed for a chosen subscription under its applicable terms and <a href="https://stripe.com/gb/privacy">privacy notice</a>. Automatic invoice email is excluded; the connected Resend service currently processes only restricted fictional test messages. Do not enter sensitive records or authorise a service requiring residency or provider commitments that this arrangement cannot meet.</p></section>
 <section id="exit"><h2>Retention, return and exit</h2><p>Current records remain while the workspace exists or until removed through the available controls. The app retains up to twenty prior database revisions and two hundred in-record activity entries; command/access metadata remains with the workspace. These are capacity limits, not timed retention rules. Archiving a customer is not erasure; contact CCS for assistance with individual erasure requests.</p><p>Managers can export current records and download a photo-inclusive encrypted backup before deletion. Exported files remain under their keeper’s control. Backups omit sign-in permissions and external billing data, and must be stored safely with passwords kept separately. Workspace deletion removes active records, memberships, invitations, database recovery history and stored photo objects; a failed object cleanup requires operator follow-up.</p><p>The customer may request record return or deletion by business email and agree an exit date. Any legally required retained data must be identified and limited to that purpose. Provider backup/security copies may follow separate deletion cycles rather than immediate erasure; CCS will request applicable deletion, put retained copies beyond ordinary use where possible and explain known limitations. No unverified fixed provider-retention period is promised. Customer-downloaded files cannot be remotely erased. No term excludes rights or liabilities that law does not permit to be excluded.</p></section>
